            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Column bound for sunny lines  \n\nIf a sunny line contained two points with the same abscissa, it would be vertical,\nhence not sunny.  Therefore a sunny line meets each column \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\))\nin **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Forced vertical lines  \n\nColumn \\(x=i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nIf \\(n+1-i>k\\) the column cannot be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines; it must be\ncovered by a non‑sunny line, i.e. by a vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\nFor every \\(i\\le n-k\\) we have \\(n+1-i>k\\); consequently the columns  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  We may take these lines to be\nexactly those vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The remaining triangular set  \n\nAfter removing the above columns, the points that are still uncovered are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a right‑angled triangle of size \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  Thus a configuration\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) can be covered by\n\\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (all the remaining lines are sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume that \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Column condition.*  \nBecause each line meets each column at most once, each column \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\))\ncontains exactly one point of the covering.  Hence the map  \n\n\\[\n\\phi:\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad\n\\phi(L_j)=\\text{column of the point of }L_j\\text{ in }\\mathcal P_k,\n\\]\n\nis a bijection.\n\n*Row condition.*  \nThe same argument applied to rows shows that the map  \n\n\\[\n\\psi:\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad\n\\psi(L_j)=\\text{row of the point of }L_j\\text{ in }\\mathcal P_k,\n\\]\n\nis also a bijection.\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) selected by the lines occupy\npairwise distinct rows and pairwise distinct columns; they form a permutation\nmatrix.  In the triangular set \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) the only such points are the\ndiagonal points \\((i,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  Hence each line \\(L_j\\) must pass\nthrough a distinct diagonal point \\((i,i)\\).\n\nConsider any two diagonal points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((j,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\nThe line through them has slope \\(1\\); therefore it also contains the point\n\\((i,j)\\).  The points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i,j)\\) lie on the same anti‑diagonal\n\\(x+y=i+j\\).  Thus this line meets the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=i+j\\) in **two**\ndifferent points, which contradicts the definition of a sunny line (a sunny\nline can intersect each anti‑diagonal at most once).  Hence such a covering of\n\\(\\mathcal P_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Existence for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  None of them is sunny and they\ncover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add the line through the points\n\\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{1-n}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\);\nhence it is sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly one\nline is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=n\\).*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\); consequently it\ncontains every point \\((i+t,1+t)\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\) as long as the sum of the\ncoordinates does not exceed \\(n+1\\).  The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are all\ndifferent and none equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is\nsunny and the lines are distinct.  A direct verification shows that every point\n\\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Hence we\nobtain a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer  \n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or jus