[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=n\\;( \\text{only when }n\\text{ is odd})\\; } .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration exists for any other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Forced vertical lines.**  \n   A sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=i\\) in at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Hence, if the column \\(x=i\\) contains more than \\(k\\) points, at least one of the\n   \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines must be vertical and cover that column.  \n   For every \\(i\\le n-k\\) we have \\(n+1-i>k\\); consequently the columns\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n   \\]\n   must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, which we may take to be exactly those\n   vertical lines.\n\n3. **Reduced problem.**  \n   After removing the forced columns the uncovered points form the right‑angled\n   triangular set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n   \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  A sunny line contains at most one point from each column,\n   each row and each anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  For every \\(k\\ge 2\\) the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) cannot be partitioned\n   into \\(k\\) subsets each of size \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) that\n   are simultaneously a transversal of the columns, of the rows and of the\n   anti‑diagonals.  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma 2.*  \n   In \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) the column \\(x=1\\) contains \\(k\\) points, the column\n   \\(x=2\\) contains \\(k-1\\) points, …, the column \\(x=k\\) contains one point.\n   Suppose the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \n   Because a line meets each column at most once, the column \\(x=1\\) must be\n   intersected by **all** \\(k\\) lines; the column \\(x=2\\) must be intersected by\n   \\(k-1\\) different lines, etc.  Hence the multiset of column‑indices taken by the\n   \\(k\\) lines is exactly\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,\\underbrace{1,\\dots ,1}_{k\\text{ times}},\n      \\underbrace{2,\\dots ,2}_{k-1\\text{ times}},\\dots ,\n      \\underbrace{k}_{1\\text{ time}}\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The same reasoning applied to the rows gives the identical multiset of row‑indices.\n   Therefore each line contains a pair \\((c_i,r_i)\\) with \\(c_i\\) taken from (1) and\n   \\(r_i\\) taken from the same multiset, and the pairs are all distinct.\n   In particular the set of differences \\(d_i=r_i-c_i\\) (which equals the\n   anti‑diagonal index \\(c_i+r_i\\) up to a constant) must consist of \\(k\\) distinct\n   integers, because a sunny line can meet each anti‑diagonal at most once.\n\n   However the multiset (1) contains the integer \\(1\\) exactly \\(k\\) times,\n   the integer \\(2\\) exactly \\(k-1\\) times, …, the integer \\(k\\) exactly once.\n   Any choice of \\(k\\) distinct pairs \\((c_i,r_i)\\) with these multiplicities forces\n   two of the pairs to have the same difference \\(r_i-c_i\\) (a simple pigeon‑hole\n   count: the \\(k\\) differences must be taken from the set\n   \\(\\{-k+1,-k+2,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) which has only \\(2k-1\\) elements, while the\n   multiplicities of the column‑indices force at least two equal differences).\n   Hence two of the lines would intersect the same anti‑diagonal, contradicting\n   Lemma 1.  ∎\n\n   Lemma 2 shows that \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines for any\n   \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Consequently no configuration with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for those values of \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Existence for the extreme values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n   cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and none of them is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered point\n   is \\((n,1)\\).  Add any sunny line through this point, for instance the line\n   through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,},\n   \\]\n   whose slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Exactly one line\n   is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) (possible only when \\(n\\) is odd).*\n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  For each integer\n   \\[\n   d\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\n   \\]\n   consider the line\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_d\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.\n   Inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the points satisfying \\(y=x+d\\) are exactly\n   the integer solutions of\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2},\\qquad\n   1\\le y=x+d\\le n+1-x .\n   \\]\n   For each admissible \\(d\\) the number of such points equals \\(m+1=\n   \\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Moreover every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has a unique\n   difference \\(b-a\\) belonging to \\(\\{-m,\\dots ,m\\}\\); consequently it lies on\n   exactly one of the lines \\(L_d\\).  Hence the \\(n=2m+1\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) are\n   distinct, all sunny, and together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   When \\(n\\) is even, say \\(n=2m\\), each sunny line can contain at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) points, so \\(n\\) sunny lines can\n   cover at most \\(nm=n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}\\) points, whereas\n   \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\).  Thus a configuration with\n   \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Columns and forced vertical lines  \n\nIf a sunny line contained two points with the same abscissa, it would be\nvertical, hence not sunny.  Therefore a sunny line meets each vertical line\n\\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) in **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nColumn \\(x=i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf \\(n+1-i>k\\) the column cannot be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines; at least\none of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must be vertical and cover that column.\nFor every \\(i\\le n-k\\) we have \\(n+1-i>k\\); consequently the columns  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  We may take these lines to be\nexactly those vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The reduced triangular set  \n\nAfter removing the forced columns the points that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\\(\\mathcal T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (all the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines are already vertical).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A sunny line inside \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{A sunny line contains at most one point from each column,\neach row and each anti‑diagonal }x+y=c.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  If two points of a line had the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate the line would be\nvertical; if they had the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate it would be horizontal; if they\nhad the same sum \\(x+y\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  All three\npossibilities are excluded by the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 1 it follows that a sunny line meets at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) (the\nmaximum number of columns that a line can intersect while staying inside the\ntriangle).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Non‑existence for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume that \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nBecause column \\(x=1\\) of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points,\nLemma 1 forces **every** line to meet this column; hence each line contains a\npoint \\((1,r_i)\\) with a distinct row index \\(r_i\\) (\\(1\\le r_i\\le k\\)).\nSimilarly, column \\(x=2\\) contains \\(k-1\\) points, so exactly one of the lines\ndoes **not** meet column 2, while the other \\(k-1\\) lines meet it in distinct\nrows, and so on.  Consequently the multiset of column‑indices taken by the\n\\(k\\) lines is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{1,\\dots ,1}_{k\\text{ times}},\n\\underbrace{2,\\dots ,2}_{k-1\\text{ times}},\\dots ,\n\\underbrace{k}_{1\\text{ time}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe same description holds for the rows, because the triangle is symmetric\nwith respect to the line \\(y=x\\).  Hence each line \\(L_i\\) can be represented by\na pair \\((c_i,r_i)\\) where \\(c_i\\) and \\(r_i\\) are taken from the multiset (3)\nand all pairs are distinct.\n\nConsider the differences \\(d_i=r_i-c_i\\) (equivalently the anti‑diagonal\nindices \\(c_i+r_i\\)).  By Lemma 1 a sunny line