 not be covered). All remaining \\(k-1\\) lines have \\(\\delta=1\\).\n\n   A line with \\(\\delta=1\\) covers the sums  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1,\\;k,\\;k-1,\\dots ,m+2,\n   \\]\n\n   while the unique \\(\\delta=2\\) line covers the sums  \n\n   \\[\n   k+1,\\;k-1,\\;k-3,\\dots ,2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\) are covered **only** by the single \\(\\delta=2\\) line.  \n   The number of points whose sum lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t=2}^{m+1}(t-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(\\delta=2\\) line contains only \\(m+1\\) points, so it can cover at most \\(m+1\\) of them.  \n   For \\(m\\ge3\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge7\\)) we have \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2>m+1\\), a contradiction.  \n   The remaining odd case is \\(k=5\\;(m=2)\\). A direct inspection shows that the only line with \\(\\delta=1\\) covering the three required sums \\(6,5,4\\) is \\(y=-2x+7\\); there is no second line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) containing three such points. Consequently the four required \\(\\delta=1\\) lines cannot exist, and \\(k=5\\) is impossible.\n\n   The only odd \\(k\\ge3\\) that survives is \\(k=3\\;(m=1)\\). An explicit covering is\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=x               &&\\text{covers }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-2x+7           &&\\text{covers }(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+4    &&\\text{covers }(1,4),(2,2),(3,0)\\ (\\text{the last point is outside }P_3),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which after discarding the extraneous point yields the three required lines  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad (1,5)-(2,3)-(3,1),\\qquad (2,1)-(1,3),\n   \\]\n\n   covering all six points of \\(P_3\\). Hence \\(k=3\\) is feasible.\n\n   Summarising the sub‑problem:\n\n   \\[\n   k\\text{ admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lifting the solution to the original triangle.**  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any admissible \\(k\\) we construct a covering of \\(P_n\\) as follows.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all unsunny).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\, (n-1)\\)).  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=-2x+(2n+1)      &&\\text{covers }(1,n),(2,n-2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+(n+\\tfrac12) &&\\text{covers }(1,n-1),(3,n-2),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=x               &&\\text{covers }(2,n-1),(1,n-2),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which together cover the six points with \\(b=n-2,n-1,n\\).  \n\n   In each case exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, and every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   By Lemma 3 the existence of a covering for a given \\(k\\) is equivalent to the existence of a covering of the reduced triangle \\(P_k\\); the sub‑problem analysis shows that only \\(k=0,1,3\\) are possible. Hence these are the only admissible values for the original problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,\\;1,\\;3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
