ets the diagonals with sums\n   \\[\n   k+1,\\;k-1,\\;k-3,\\dots ,2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence the diagonals whose sums belong to the set\n   \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) are covered **only** by the single type B line.\n   The number of points whose sum lies in this set equals\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{t=2}^{m+1}(t-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The type B line contains only \\(m+1\\) points, so it can cover at most\n   \\(m+1\\) of the points counted in (6).  For \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge5\\))\n   we have \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2>m+1\\), a contradiction.  Therefore the only\n   admissible odd value is \\(k=3\\) (\\(m=1\\)).\n\n   For \\(k=3\\) an explicit covering is\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&: y=x               &&\\text{covers }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_{2}&: y=-2x+7           &&\\text{covers }(1,5),(2,3),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+4    &&\\text{covers }(1,4),(2,2),(3,0),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   and after discarding the extraneous point \\((3,0)\\) we obtain the three\n   required sunny lines covering all six points of \\(P_{3}\\).\n\n   Summarising the sub‑problem:\n\n   \\[\n   k\\text{ admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lifting the solution to the original triangle.**  \n   Let \\(n\\ge3\\) be arbitrary and let \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all unsunny).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together\n   with the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\,(n-1)\\)).\n   Every point of \\(P_{n}\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\) and the\n   three sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&: y=-2x+(2n+1),\\\\\n   L_{2}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+(n+\\tfrac12),\\\\\n   L_{3}&: y=x .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks directly that these three lines contain respectively the\n   point‑sets\n\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,n),(2,n-2)\\},\\qquad\n   \\{(1,n-1),(3,n-2)\\},\\qquad\n   \\{(2,n-1),(1,n-2)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which together with the horizontal lines cover all points of \\(P_{n}\\).\n\n   Because of the reduction proved in step 3, the existence of a covering for\n   a given \\(k\\) is equivalent to the existence of a covering of \\(P_{k}\\);\n   (7) shows that only \\(k=0,1,3\\) are possible.  Hence these are the only\n   admissible values for the original problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,\\;1,\\;3.}\n\\]"
