\(|\\Delta s|=1\\) or \\(|\\Delta s|=2\\).\n\n3. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Suppose \\(P_{n}\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Replace the unsunny lines by the diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   x+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1),\n   \\]\n   which are unsunny and together cover all points whose sum exceeds \\(k+1\\).\n   Consequently the original \\(k\\) sunny lines already cover all points of\n   \\[\n   P_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to the **sub‑problem**\n\n   > *For which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) does there exist a covering of\n   > \\(P_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?*\n\n4. **Solution of the sub‑problem.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Trivial – no point has to be covered.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) (or any sunny line through\n   \\((1,1)\\)) covers the unique point of \\(P_{1}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \\(P_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  Any line containing two of\n   these points is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); all are\n   unsunny.  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover \\(P_{2}\\).\n\n   *\\(k\\ge3\\).*  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(P_{k}\\).  Since\n   \\(|P_{k}|=k(k+1)/2\\), equality must hold in Lemma 1 for every sunny line.\n   Consequently \\(k\\) is odd; write \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  Then each\n   sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points and, by the proof of Lemma 1,\n   satisfies \\(|q-p|=1\\) or \\(|q-p|=2\\).\n\n   – If \\(|q-p|=1\\) (type A) the sums of the points on the line are the\n   consecutive integers\n   \\[\n   m+2,\\;m+3,\\dots ,2m+2\\;(=k+1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence a type A line meets **every** diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with\n   \\(s\\in\\{m+2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) exactly once.\n\n   – If \\(|q-p|=2\\) (type B) all sums have the same parity as \\(k+1\\)\n   (which is even); the line meets only the even diagonals and contains\n   \\(m+1\\) even‑sum points.\n\n   Let \\(x\\) be the number of type B lines; the remaining \\(k-x\\) lines are\n   type A.  The total number of points on even diagonals equals\n   \\[\n   E=1+3+5+\\dots +k=\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}=(m+1)^{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   A type B line contributes \\(m+1\\) even points; a type A line contributes\n   \\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil\\) even points (the number of even integers in\n   the block (3)).  Hence\n   \\[\n   x(m+1)+(k-x)\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=(m+1)^{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(m\\) is even* then \\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil=\\frac{m+2}{2}\\) and\n   (5) gives \\(x=-1\\), impossible.  Thus no solution exists for\n   \\(k\\equiv1\\pmod4\\) with \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n   *If \\(m\\) is odd* then \\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil=\\frac{m+1}{2}\\) and\n   (5) yields \\(x=1\\).  Hence exactly one type B line is present and the\n   other \\(k-1=2m\\) lines are of type A.\n\n   For a type A line the smallest sum occurring is \\(m+2\\); the diagonal\n   \\(x+y=m+2\\) contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points.  Since there are \\(2m\\)\n   type A lines, we would need at least \\(2m\\) distinct points on that\n   diagonal, which is impossible when \\(m\\ge2\\) because \\(2m>m+1\\).\n   Consequently \\(m\\le1\\), i.e. \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n   Summarising the sub‑problem:\n   \\[\n   k\\text{ admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lifting the solution to the original triangle.**  \n   Let \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the sunny line through\n   \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), namely\n   \\[\n   y=-(n-1)x+2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the three sunny lines that cover \\(P_{3}\\):\n   