                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_{n}=|P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **unsunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Two elementary lemmas  \n\nWrite a line in primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\np x+q y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on the line are obtained from one particular solution\n\\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0}+qt,\\;y_{0}-pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence the sum \\(s=x+y\\) varies as  \n\n\\[\ns=(x_{0}+y_{0})+(q-p)t .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(d=q-p\\).  \nFor a **sunny** line we have \\(d\\neq0\\); for an **unsunny** line\n\\(d=0\\) (horizontal or vertical) or the line is already of the form\n\\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf \\(L\\) is sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap P_{n}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) be a point of \\(L\\cap P_{n}\\) with the smallest\npossible sum \\(s_{\\min}=x_{0}+y_{0}\\).  \nFrom (3) every other point of \\(L\\cap P_{n}\\) has sum  \n\n\\[\ns=s_{\\min}+d\\,t\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;s\\le n+1,\\;s\\ge2).\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(t\\) form an integer interval\n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}\\le t\\le t_{\\max},\\qquad \nt_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2-s_{\\min}}{d}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\;\nt_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-s_{\\min}}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap P_{n}|=t_{\\max}-t_{\\min}+1\n          \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-s_{\\min}}{|d|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n                -\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2-s_{\\min}}{|d|}\\Bigr\\rceil+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_{0},y_{0}\\ge1\\) we have \\(s_{\\min}\\ge2\\); moreover the\npositivity of the coordinates forces \\(s_{\\min}\\ge|d|+2\\) (otherwise the\nnext step in the direction of decreasing \\(t\\) would make one coordinate\nnon‑positive).  Substituting \\(s_{\\min}\\ge|d|+2\\) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap P_{n}|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(|d|+2)}{|d|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n            =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|d|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe right–hand side is maximal when \\(|d|=1\\) or \\(|d|=2\\); in both cases it\nequals \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence (4) holds.\nMoreover, equality in (4) forces \\(|d|=1\\) or \\(|d|=2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf \\(L\\) is unsunny then \\(|L\\cap P_{n}|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAn unsunny line is either \\(x=c\\) (vertical), \\(y=c\\) (horizontal) or\n\\(x+y=c\\) (diagonal).  In the first two cases the line meets at most\n\\(n\\) points of the triangle because the other coordinate can take the\nvalues \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  In the diagonal case the points satisfy\n\\(a+b=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) such\npoints. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nAssume that for a given \\(n\\ge3\\) the set \\(P_{n}\\) can be covered by\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nLet the unsunny lines be \\(U_{1},\\dots ,U_{n-k}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal\nline  \n\n\\[\nD_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are unsunny, they are all\ndifferent, and together they contain precisely the points of \\(P_{n}\\)\nwhose sum exceeds \\(k+1\\).  Indeed, a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) lies\non exactly one of those diagonals, and the number of points on the\ndiagonal \\(x+y=s\\) equals \\(s-1\\).\n\nConsequently we may **replace** the original unsunny lines\n\\(U_{1},\\dots ,U_{n-k}\\) by the set \\(\\{D_{s}\\mid k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\)\nwithout losing any covered point: the new family still consists of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and now\ncovers all points with sum \\(>k+1\\).\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines must already cover every point of  \n\n\\[\nP_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem.\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  \n> For which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) does there exist a covering of\n> \\(P_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Solving the sub‑problem  \n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  Trivial – no point has to be covered.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) (or any sunny line through\n\\((1,1)\\)) covers the unique point of \\(P_{1}\\).\n\n*\\(k=2\\).*  \\(P_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line containing two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\); all are unsunny.  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover \\(P_{2}\\).\n\n*\\(k\\ge3\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(P_{k}\\).  Since  \n\n\\[\n|P_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwe must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) were even, the left–hand side equals \\(k\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}=k^{2}/2\\),\nwhich is strictly smaller than the right–hand side for \\(k\\ge2\\); thus\n\\(k\\) cannot be even.  Hence \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\) with\n\\(m\\ge1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (8) becomes\n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2,\n\\]\n\ni.e. equality holds.  Consequently **every** sunny line must attain the\nmaximum in Lemma 1, and therefore each of them contains exactly\n\\(m+1\\) points of \\(P_{k}\\) and satisfies \\(|q-p|=1\\) or \\(|q-p|=2