n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4.   The diagonal \\(x+y=m+2\\)  \n\nFor a type A line the smallest sum occurring is \\(m+2\\); the diagonal\n\\(x+y=m+2\\) contains precisely \\(m+1\\) points of \\(P_{k}\\).  Since we\nhave \\(2m\\) type A lines, each of them must meet this diagonal in a\ndifferent point.  This is possible only when  \n\n\\[\n2m\\le m+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad m\\le1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m=1\\) we obtain \\(k=2m+1=3\\); if \\(m=0\\) we obtain \\(k=1\\), which\nhas already been treated.  Hence **the only admissible odd values are**\n\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).\n\nCollecting the results of the sub‑problem we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\text{ admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lifting the solution to the original triangle  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are unsunny and together they contain every point of\n\\(P_{n}\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the\nsunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\),\n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=-(n-1)x+2n-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) has slope \\(-(n-1)\\neq0,-1\\), hence it is sunny; the horizontal\nlines cover all points with \\(b\\le n-1\\), while \\(L\\) covers the remaining\npoints (in particular the point \\((1,n)\\)).\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  \nFirst cover the small triangle \\(P_{3}\\) with three sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-2x+5,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-\\frac12 x+\\frac52 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThese three lines are sunny (their slopes are \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\)) and\nthey contain respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\\;\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nhence they cover all six points of \\(P_{3}\\).\n\nNow add the unsunny diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad (s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are \\(n-3\\) distinct lines.  They cover every point of \\(P_{n}\\) whose\nsum exceeds \\(4\\); the three sunny lines (13) cover all points with sum\n\\(\\le4\\).  Consequently all points of \\(P_{n}\\) are covered and exactly\nthree of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Conclusion  \n\nThe reduction of §2 shows that a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nexists for the original triangle \\(P_{n}\\) **iff** a covering of\n\\(P_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  By the analysis of §3 the latter\nis possible precisely for \\(k=0,1,3\\).  The explicit constructions of\n§4 exhibit coverings for these three values for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,\\;1,\\;3.}\n\\]"
