r \\(|\\Delta s|=2\\).  Indeed, for \\(|\\Delta s|\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta s|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThus the maximal possible value of \\(|L\\cap P_{n}|\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{|\\Delta s|\\ge 1}\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta s|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nwhich proves (3). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf \\(L\\) is unsunny then \\(|L\\cap P_{n}|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAn unsunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or of the\nform \\(x+y=c\\).  In each case the integer points of \\(L\\) inside the triangle\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) have distinct first (or second) coordinates ranging from\n\\(1\\) to \\(n\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points lie on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nAssume that \\(P_{n}\\) can be covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny.  Let the \\(n-k\\) unsunny lines be \\(U_{1},\\dots ,U_{n-k}\\).\n\nFor every integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum exceeds \\(k+1\\) lies on exactly one of the\ndiagonals \\(D_{s}\\) (its sum is that \\(s\\)).  There are precisely\n\\(n-(k+1)=n-k\\) such values of \\(s\\); consequently we can replace the\nunsunny lines \\(U_{i}\\) by the distinct diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\)\nwithout losing any point of \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum is \\(\\ge k+2\\).\n\nHence the original \\(k\\) sunny lines already cover all points whose sum is\n\\(\\le k+1\\); i.e. they cover  \n\n\\[\nP_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem.\n\n> **Sub‑problem.**  \n> For which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) does there exist a covering of\n> \\(P_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Solving the sub‑problem  \n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  Trivial – no point has to be covered.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) (or any sunny line through\n\\((1,1)\\)) contains the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(P_{1}\\).\n\n*\\(k=2\\).*  \\(P_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\);\nall are unsunny, so two sunny lines cannot cover \\(P_{2}\\).\n\n*\\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(P_{k}\\).  Since  \n\n\\[\n|P_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nthe inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\nforces equality in Lemma 1 for **every** sunny line.  Consequently\n\n* \\(k\\) must be odd; write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge 1\\);\n* each sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(P_{k}\\);\n* by the equality case of Lemma 1 we have \\(|\\Delta s|=|q-p|=1\\) or \\(2\\).\n\nWe distinguish the two possibilities.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Type A lines (\\(|q-p|=1\\))\n\nFor such a line the sums of its points form an arithmetic progression with\nstep \\(\\pm1\\).  Starting from the smallest sum \\(s_{\\min}=m+2\\) (the smallest\npossible sum of a point of \\(P_{k}\\) lying on a sunny line) the progression\nreaches the maximal sum \\(s_{\\max}=k+1=2m+2\\).  Hence the set of sums is  \n\n\\[\n\\{m+2,m+3,\\dots ,2m+2\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus **every** diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{m+2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) is met\n**exactly once** by a type A line.  (The “exactly once’’ follows because the\nstep is \\(\\pm1\\); two different points on the same line cannot have the same\nsum.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Type B lines (\\(|q-p|=2\\))\n\nNow the sums change by \\(\\pm2\\); consequently all points of such a line have\nthe same parity as the initial sum.  Since \\(k+1=2m+2\\) is even, a type B\nline meets only the **even** diagonals among those listed in (9) and contains\nexactly \\(m+1\\) even‑sum points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Counting even points  \n\nLet \\(x\\) be the number of type B lines; the remaining \\(k-x\\) lines are of\ntype A.  The total number of points of \\(P_{k}\\) lying on even diagonals is  \n\n\\[\nE=1+3+5+\\dots +k=\\Bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)^{2}=(m+1)^{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA type B line contributes \\(m+1\\) even points, while a type A line contributes\n\\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil\\) even points (the number of even integers in the\nblock (9)).  Hence\n\n\\[\nx(m+1)+(k-x)\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=(m+1)^{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m\\) is even* then \\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil=\\frac{m+2}{2}\\).  \nSubstituting \\(k=2m+1\\) into (11) gives  \n\n\\[\nx(m+1)+(2m+1-x)\\frac{m+2}{2}=(m+1)^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Therefore **no solution exists when \\(m\\) is even**, i.e.\nwhen \\(k\\equiv1\\pmod 4\\) and \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is odd* then \\(\\lceil\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rceil=\\frac{m+1}{2}\\).  \nEquation (11) becomes  \n\n\\[\nx(m+1)+(2m+1-x)\\frac{m+1}{2}=(m+1)^{2}\\Longrightarrow x=1.\n\\]\n\nThus exactly one type B line is present and the other \\(k-1=2m\\) lines are\ntype A.\n\nConsider the diagonal \\(x+y=m+2\\).  It contains precisely \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(P_{k}\\).  Each type A line meets this diagonal exactly once (by (9)), so the\n\\(2m\\) type A lines would require \\(2m\\) distinct points on that diagonal.\nHence we must have \\(2m\\le m+1\\), i.e. \\(m\\le1\\).  Because \\(m\\ge1\\), we obtain\n\\(m=1\\) and consequently \\(k=2m+1=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSummarising the sub‑problem we have proved  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non‑negative integers for which \\(P_{k}\\) can be covered\nby exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lifting the solution to the original triangle  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n  \\]\n\n  All of them are unsunny, and every point of \\(P_{n}\\) lies on the line\n  whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals its second coordinate.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together\n  with the sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\),\n\n  \\[\n  y=-(n-1)x+2n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The horizontal lines cover all points with \\(b\\le n-1\\); the remaining\n  points have sum \\(n+1\\) and lie on the added sunny line.\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  The three sunny lines that cover \\(P_{3}\\) are  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{1}:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_{2}:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  L_{3}:\\;x+2y=7 .\n  \\]\n\n  Each of them contains exactly the points of \\(P_{3}\\) (and possibly\n  additional points with larger sum).  Add the unsunny diagonal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=s\\qquad(s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1),\n  \\]\n\n  which are \\(n-3\\) in number.  These diagonals cover every point of\n  \\(P_{n}\\) whose sum exceeds \\(4\\); the three sunny lines cover all points\n  with sum \\(\\le4\\).  Hence all points of \\(P_{n}\\) are covered and exactly\n  three lines are sunny.\n\nBecause of the reduction proved in Step 2, the existence of a covering for a\ngiven \\(k\\) is equivalent to the existence of a covering of \\(P_{k}\\); (12)\nshows that only \\(k=0,1,3\\) are possible.  Therefore these are the only\nadmissible numbers of sunny lines for the original problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible num